# Deliberately empty.
#
# Bun auto-loads `.env` for `bun test`, so a developer's real credentials —
# COMPOSIO_API_KEY and friends — land in the test process. Tests written to
# assert the *unconfigured* path then see a configured one and fail, but only
# on machines that run the app; CI has no `.env`, so it stays green and the
# failure looks like "my branch is broken".
#
# Worse than the flakiness: a test that reads a live broker key can make real
# API calls from a local run.
#
# Passing this file via `--env-file` replaces that auto-load. Tests set the
# environment they need explicitly, which is what they already do.
#
# Two things it does NOT cover, both by design rather than oversight:
#
#   - It binds to the invocation, not to `bun test`. `bun run test:unit` and
#     `bun run test:integration` pass the flag; a bare `bun test <file>` — the
#     usual way to chase one failure — does not, and still loads `.env`. Pass
#     `--env-file=test/.env.test` yourself when running a file directly.
#   - It replaces the dotenv load; it does not clear the environment. A value
#     exported in your shell (direnv and friends) already outranks `.env` and
#     is unaffected either way.
#
# There is no repo-wide switch for this. `bunfig.toml` has no env-file key, and
# a `[test]` preload that scrubbed the environment would also hit `bun run
# eval`, which legitimately needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from `.env`.
